\par\par \b \uc2  Peru's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Sells Dollars To Bolster Sol \b0\par\par\uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2001\par 18:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  LIMA -(Dow Jones)- The Central Reserve Bank of Peru stepped into the money market Wednesday, selling dollars for the first time in over a year to bolster the sol, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said. \par \par \uc2  At 0500 GMT the nuevo sol was trading at PEN3.5880 to the dollar on a spread of PEN3.5860 to PEN 3.5900. \par \par \uc2  The nuevo sol had reached levels of PEN3.603 to the dollar in early morning trade on local electoral worries and regional instability led by Argentina, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The nuevo sol had closed Tuesday at a mid-rate of PEN3.5950 a dollar on a spread of PEN3.5940-PEN3.5960, local traders said. \par \par \uc2  "We are in the market and we will continue to sell as much as necessary," a trader said, declining to give a maximum amount of dollars to be sold. \par \par \uc2  Local traders, however, pegged the amount at about $15 million. \par \par \uc2  Peru's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weakened notably after the first round of presidential elections held April 8. No candidate took the majority, and a second round of voting will be held in late May or early June. \par \par \uc2  Managing Director Javier de la Rocha said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step in to defend the sol if needed, local newspaper Gestion reported Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "When we think it's opportune, we will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  De la Rocha added that \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not control the sol, and only steps into the market to avoid abrupt fluctuations in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Peru officially has a "dirty float," and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps a tight hold on liquidity. \par \par \uc2  The sol's recent depreciation occurred because of the overall Latin American situation and the uncertainty related to any electoral process, de la Rocha added. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Javier Silva Ruete said the sol's recent depreciation should not be cause for worry, Gestion reported Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "There has not been a substantial change in the sol. It will drop again a few cents and settle within the broad range of the dirty float," Silva Ruete said. \par \par \uc2  He added that this system had been highly successful over the last ten years. \par \par \uc2  Although jitters are not likely to subside before the runoff vote, Deutsche Bank analysts see the sol's depreciation "as limited to levels around 3.63/3.64 before the second round." \par \par \uc2  According to the report, Peru's sol is supported by a dollarization rate of nearly 60%, a strong position in reserves, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tight hold on liquidity. \par \par \uc2  "Of course, a Garcia victory would trigger further volatility," Deutsche Bank adds in reference to presidential candidate Alan Garcia, whose tumultuous relationship with the financial community is unlikely to soothe market nerves. \par \par \uc2  -By Miriam Gerace, Dow Jones Newswires; 511-221-7050; peru@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1071 1837 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx4p01w17\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
