\par\par \b \uc2  Jitters in Peru as Fujimori makes surprise US trip. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jude Webber \par \uc2 823 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2000\par 19:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LIMA, Sept 28 (Reuters) - Peruvian President Alberto Fujimori's surprise trip to Washington perplexed opposition politicians on Thursday as talk of a coup plot at home sent the sol falling against the dollar to its lowest level this year. \par \par \uc2  Fujimori left for talks with the head of the region's top diplomatic body, the Organisation of American States (OAS), in the early hours of the morning. \par \par \uc2  The State Department said Fujimori had requested a meeting with senior U.S. officials, but no details were available. Fujimori's office said only he would stay until Friday. \par \par \uc2  "(Fujimori's trip) is inexplicable at a time when we need a strong government presence," opposition congressman Valentino Paniagua told local RPP radio. \par \par \uc2  The president, plunged into crisis by a corruption scandal involving his former spy chief, Vladimiro Montesinos, has announced early elections in which he will not run, and last week, appeared to have calmed a volatile situation in which coup fears were rife by rallying the military behind him. \par \par \uc2  However, on Wednesday, a congressmen defecting from his Peru 2000 alliance said the military had urged legislators to break away and form a new group in Congress to sow chaos and pave the way for a coup by pro-Montesinos military within weeks. \par \par \uc2  The army dismissed the talk on Wednesday as "absolutely false", but in the current jittery climate, any talk of military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked panic. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  is down because of people speculating that Fujimori left the country because he had to. I don't believe that rumour. The military denied any kind of coup activity," said one New York bond trader, as Peru Brady bonds prices fell. \par \par \uc2  Some political analysts said that although there were probably divisions in the armed forces, Fujimori appeared to have rallied them behind him and perhaps intended his trip to show he was confident and in control of the situation at home. \par \par \uc2  Montesinos was bundled out of the country to Panama at the weekend after pressure from the United States, the OAS and regional powers. He is awaiting news of his asylum request. \par \par \uc2  Montesinos, who headed Peru's notorious spy service amid allegations of corruption and human rights abuses, was seen in a video aired on television allegedly bribing an opposition legislator $15,000 to switch sides. State prosecutors closed an investigation into the affair before he fled to Panama. \par \par \uc2  Political analysts said it appeared Montesinos, who is still believed to have friends in high places in the military and the judiciary, had cut an impunity deal before leaving. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Fujimori's ruling alliance has begun to splinter. Departures and defections in past days have left him without a majority in Congress for the first time since 1992. \par \par \uc2  FINANCIAL MARKETS WORRIED \par \par \uc2  Nerves - and questions about why Fujimori should suddenly need to travel to Washington - unsettled financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The Peruvian sol sank one percent to 3.5300 to the dollar - its lowest level of the year - at the opening, compared with Wednesday's close of 3.5035 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The market is very nervous about Fujimori leaving for Washington and continued tension over the late interest payment on Peru's Brady bongs," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer. \par \par \uc2  Peru, which is embroiled in a legal battle with one of its U.S. creditors, has until Oct. 7 to pay an $80 million interest bill or be officially declared in default - a move that would weigh on its chances of raising foreign capital. \par \par \uc2  Peru has the money to pay the interest bill but has delayed payment because of a legal ruling that hedge fund Elliott Associates could seize any funds to settle a pending claim. \par \par \uc2  Peru Brady bond prices in New York, which have been under pressure since Fujimori's announcement of elections almost two weeks ago, fell by up to one point while the rest of the market was buoyant. \par \par \uc2  Lima's stock market fell 0.5 percent in scant volume in early trade coloured by the political confusion. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers did not rule out more drops in the sol. "The talk about military coups is not helping, this will add fuel to the fire," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer, adding the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  FUJIMORI FLIES ABROAD - BUT WHY NOW? \par \par \uc2  Opposition congressmen were incredulous that Fujimori should have chosen this moment to leave Peru. \par \par \uc2  "Right at the peak of the crisis he goes to Washington to support (OAS Secretary-General Cesar) Gaviria because he's got 23 presidents wondering why (Gaviria) wrote a letter urging asylum for Montesinos," said Luis Solari, spokesman for the Peru Posible party of main opposition leader Alejandro Toledo. \par \par \uc2  Gaviria, backed by other leaders in the hemisphere and the United States, which feared Peruvian democracy was under threat, urged Panama to take in Montesinos to calm a potentially volatile situation. (Additional reporting by Hugh Bronstein in New York). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9s06a1o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
