\par\par \b \uc2  Peru's Sol Weaker; Fears On Argentina Continues \b0\par\par\uc2 210 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 1999\par 21:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's nuevo sol finished weaker Thursday as banks continued buying dollars despite denials of rumors that Argentina might consider devaluing its peso, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The nuevo sol closed at a mid-rate of PEN3.3260 per dollar on a spread of PEN3.3245-PEN3.3275 per dollar, local traders said. \par \par \uc2  That is weaker than Wednesday's close at PEN3.3235 per dollar on a spread of PEN3.3225-PEN3.3245 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Although rumors of a possible devaluation in Argentina were ruled out, investors continued to fear any impact on the sol and so they bought dollars," said a trader at Banco Wiese. \par \par \uc2  The Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market because "investors were buying dollars and there were enough soles," an official at \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  The key overnight lending rate was around 11.0%, traders said, higher than Wednesday's official closing figure at 10.1%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As of May 19 the outstanding value of CD's, used by the government to\par control the money supply and influence the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was PEN760.0\par million.\par -By Virginia Rey-Sanchez; 511-221-7050;\par  VReySanchez@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 20-05-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1948GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv5k03vr5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
