\par\par \b \uc2  Peru cenbank sells dollars, first time in 4 years. \b0\par\par\uc2 420 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 1998\par 21:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LIMA, Sept 8 (Reuters) - Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars Tuesday for the first time in four years in efforts to prop up its depreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid world market woes spurred by Russia's near economic meltdown. \par \par \uc2  The bank sold $25 million, helping the Peruvian sol to end trading at 3.057/3.060 against the dollar, compared with 3.062/3.065 at the close Monday, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still well below pre-Russia crisis levels. \par \par \uc2  Peru's free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically depreciates only gradually - roughly 0.001 sol within a given session. But since August 26, the sol has slid 0.075 sol/0.076 sol against the dollar when Russia's crisis ignited falls on markets worldwide. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said last week it may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. Official government policy is only to smooth over sharp changes in the sol's value. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  generally influences the exchange rate through controlling liquidity by selling Certificates of Deposits (CDs) or auctioning overnight repurchase agreements, known as repos. \par \par \uc2  The government has also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would buy an average of more than $90 million per month this year in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depreciate gradually. \par \par \uc2  This year's Asia and Russia crises have caused the sol to depreciate more sharply this year as many investors have left Lima's slumping stock exchange and sought a safe haven in dollars, adding to the demand for greenbacks. \par \par \uc2  Still, because Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is free-floating, meaning its value is not propped up by control mechanisms, the sol is regarded as less sensitive to a risk of devaluation than other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which operate within a band system, local macroeconomic analysts said. \par \par \uc2  In its 1998 budget, the government forecast an average exchange rate of 2.90 soles to the dollar and set a formal target that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would close the year at 2.95 soles per dollar. \par \par \uc2  As early as July, President Alberto Fujimori predicted the turmoil on global markets would force the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the 3.00 soles barrier. \par \par \uc2  In the 1999 budget sent to Congress last month, the government estimated next year's average exchange rate would be 3.10 soles to the dollar. Some economists, however, expect it to fall to an average of 3.25 soles. \par \par \uc2  ((Alistair Scrutton, Lima newsroom, Tel: 511 221-2134, Fax: 511 221-2133, lima.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9800xga\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
