\par\par \b \uc2  Peru cenbank says to act if sol falls too sharply. \b0\par\par\uc2 471 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 1998\par 23:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LIMA, Sep 2 (Reuters) - Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slide over the last few weeks amid world market woes is only temporary, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will act to brake particularly sharp movements, the bank's president German Suarez said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "We are sure there will be a greater supply of dollars and this (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall) is temporary. But if there is too much nervousness, or brusque and speculative changes the bank has to act," Suarez told reporters, in his first public statements after chaos in Russia last month first sparked fears Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be hit. \par \par \uc2  Peru's free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically depreciates only gradually - roughly 0.001 sol within a given session - but has seen steep falls in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  In six sessions since Aug. 26, when an economic crisis in Russia ignited falls on markets worldwide, the sol has fallen 0.69/0.70 sol against the dollar to stand at 3.040/3.044. \par \par \uc2  Since the near Russian economic meltdown last week, \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market while official government policy is only to smooth over sharp changes in the sol's value, according to local traders. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  generally influences the exchange rate through controlling liquidity by selling Certificates of Deposits (CDs) or auctioning overnight repurchase agreements, known as repos. \par \par \uc2  The government has also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would buy an average of more than $90 million per month this year of the sol in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depreciate gradually. \par \par \uc2  But this year's Asia and Russia crises have caused the sol to depreciate more sharply this year as many investors have left Lima's slumping stock exchange and sought a safe haven in dollars, adding to the demand for greenback. \par \par \uc2  Still, because Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is free-floating, meaning its value is not propped up by control mechanisms, the sol is regarded as less sensitive to a risk of devaluation than other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which operate within a band system, local macroeconomic analysts said. \par \par \uc2  In its 1998 budget, the government forecast an average exchange rate of 2.90 soles to the dollar and set a formal target that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would close the year at 2.95 soles per dollar. \par \par \uc2  As early as July, President Alberto Fujimori predicted the turmoil on global markets would force the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the 3.00 soles barrier. \par \par \uc2  In the 1999 budget sent to Congress this month, the government estimated next year's average exchange rate would be 3.10 soles to the dollar. Some economists, however, expect it to fall to an average of 3.25 soles. \par \par \uc2  ((Eduardo Orozco, Lima newsroom, Tel: 511 221-2134, Fax: 511 221-2133, lima.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du92021af\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
