\par\par \b \uc2  Peru Sol Stronger Ahead Of Approaching Tax Deadline \b0\par\par\uc2 304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 1998\par 22:19\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's nuevo sol finished stronger for the first time in seven sessions Wednesday, as traders said that private banks bought dollars in anticipation of an approaching tax deadline that must be paid in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The sol finished the session selling at 2.800 nuevos soles per dollar, higher than Tuesday's close at 2.808 soles per dollar. The buy-rate for the sol Wednesday was 2.796 soles per dollar, stronger than 2.805 soles per dollar Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Banks sold dollars preventing further depreciation because the close proximity of a tax payment deadline," a trader at Contibolsa, a local brokerage. "That pushed the sol up." \par \par \uc2  The tax deadline referred to starts for most companies around mid-month. \par \par \uc2  The trader also said that Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market by buying or selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  But investors who bought 60 million soles in 28-day certificates of deposit on Tuesday paid for them on Wednesday, using up some of the supply of soles. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't buy dollars on the market, traders said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sometimes buys dollars to weaken the sol and make exports more attractive. \par \par \uc2  The 24-hour interbank interest rate, the lending rate between banks, was around 16.3% Wednesday, higher than Tuesday's rate at 16.2%, and higher than the typical rate of 13% to 14%. The official 24-hour rate for one day isn't released until the following day. The trader said the high interest rate reflected the short supply of soles. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the informal market, the dollar maintained its value at 2.80 soles.\par    -By Virginia Rey-Sanchez; 511-221-7050;VReySanchez@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  11-02-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2119GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1071 1837 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010917du2b0021g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
