\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru \highlight\uc2  Sol End Weaker As  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 186 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Dezember 1997\par 22:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LIMA (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nuevo sol finished weaker against the U.S. dollar Wednesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the exchange rate, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dealers said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  used the usual buyback of Certificates of Deposit to introduce local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The sol finished the session selling at 2.728 soles per dollar from 2.725 soles per dollar Tuesday. The buy-rate for the sol Wednesday was 2.723 soles per dollar compared to 2.715 soles per dollar the previous session. \par \par \uc2  'The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  introduced soles by buying back 120 million soles of Certificates of Deposits issued by the institution,' a trader with Banco Continental said. \par \par \uc2  The trader added that \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't buy dollars in the local market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sometimes buys dollars to weaken the sol and make Peruvian exports more attractive. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the informal market, the dollar closed unchanged at 2.72 soles per\par dollar.\par -By Virginia Rey-Sanchez, 511-221-7050;VReySanchez@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 24-12-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2140GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1071 1837 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtco09x0a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
