\par\par \b \uc2  Peru/ \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  -2: Continued Dollar Buying Expected \b0\par\par\uc2 181 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 1997\par 14:39\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  periodically buys dollars in order to weaken the sol and make Peruvian exports more attractive. \par \par \uc2  So far, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts have had minimal impact, as the sol strengthened Tuesday to 2.624 per dollar, the same as Wednesday's opening level. The sol closed Monday at 2.625 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The sol has now strengthened or maintained value in 10 of the last 12 sessions since reaching its weakest point on record, 2.651 per dollar, on Feb. 3. \par \par \uc2  Traders contacted Wednesday say they expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue buying dollars when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets open Wednesday at 1430 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's periodic \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are seen as an effort to help \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  curb its trade deficit, which was $1.94 billion in 1996, down slightly from a record $2.14 billion in 1995. But the actions often draw fire from opponents, who say it erodes wages and contributes to inflation. \par \par \uc2  -Eric J. Lyman \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES EMERGING MARKETS REPORT 19-02-97 \par \par \uc2  1339GMT \par \par \uc2  1802 1837 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020011006dt2j002qk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
