\par\par \b \uc2  Exotic  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2 /Peru -4: IMF Targets A Consideration \b0\par\par\uc2 400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 1996\par 01:55\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Secada says the government's dollar-buying also reflects a broader change now underway in how the government of President Alberto Fujimori adjusts its economic policy to meet the requirements of its letter of intent with the International Monetary Fund (IMF), Secada says. \par \par \uc2  On May 11, the government published a draft of the letter of intent, which spelled out Peru's GDP growth, inflation, fiscal spending and structural reform targets for the next three years. \par \par \uc2  The dollars that Peru accumulates will be an important component in Peru's ability to meet its key fiscal target in 1996, a 1% of GDP primary fiscal surplus, Secada says. \par \par \uc2  But these dollars are being accumulated by the government mainly to make future external debt payments, he says. "This is not a coordinated move to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  prefers to concentrate its policy efforts on fine-tuning adjustments in \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary base, not in large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Secada says. \par \par \uc2  Another reason why the government would be unlikely to either allow, or push, the sol to depreciate faster is to protect Peruvian companies and the government from sharp increases in the payments they face on their dollar-denominated debts. \par \par \uc2  "That provides a strong disincentive to devaluing the sol," Secada says. \par \par \uc2  But that doesn't mean that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders in London, New York and Lima will be able to rest easy. \par \par \uc2  According to one senior trader in Lima, contradictory \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecasts by senior economic policy makers "caused a lot of confusion among analysts about where the government sees the sol heading." \par \par \uc2  In February, Peru \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist Henry Uruga said an overvaluation of the sol was having a negative impact on Peru's trade balance, and added that the sol should trade at about 2.55 soles to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  One month later, Finance Minister Jorge Camet, speaking to reporters in Buenos Aires during a meeting of the Inter-American Development Bank (IDB) meeting, roiled Lima markets when he said the sol would drop to 2.65 soles per dollar by year end. \par \par \uc2  "I can't believe (Camet) meant that," the Lima trader says. "We started the year with high inflation due to rising agricultural prices, and there's just not the monetary conditions to support that much depreciation." \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES EMERGING MARKETS REPORT 05-23-96 \par \par \uc2  1955EDT \par \par \uc2  1838 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020011013ds5n009ph\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
